  "notes": "The drill chassis under a sped-up R&B sample. The two-bar snare form survives — beat 3 in the first bar, beat 4 in the second — but everything around it softens: snaps rather than a big reverb snare, triplet hats played at half the density and well below full velocity, one or two rolls where Brooklyn has four, and a warm 808 that glides less and sits on the chord bottom instead of running its own counter-riff. The harmony is what actually names the lane: four-chord loops with real ii–v–i motion voiced as sevenths and ninths, where drill is a two-chord minor vamp of triads. The topline is sung rather than plucked — denser, smoother and higher than a drill bell line, because it is standing in for a vocal chop.",
